<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-seqs-convergence-properties" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Convergence properties</title>

  <p>In this section we shall establish and discuss several properties of sequence convergence, i.e. common or general ways that convergent sequences behave, and how they interact with the operations of addition and multiplication that we can apply to sequences. Many of the ideas behind these properties will re-occur later in the context of the convergence of series, function values and properties of differentiation and integration.</p>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-seq-unique-limit">
    <title>Uniqueness of limits</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        If a sequence converges then its limit is unique, i.e. if <m>x_n \to L</m> and <m>x_n \to M</m> as <m>n \to \infty</m>, then <m>L=M</m>.    
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
<p>
					Suppose that a sequence <m>a_n</m> converges to limits <m>L , M \in \mathbb{R}</m>. If we assume that <m>L</m> and <m>M</m> are distinct, i.e. that <m>|L - M|&gt;0</m>, then we can let <m>\epsilon = |L - M| / 2 &gt; 0</m>. Since <m>a_n \to L</m> there exists an <m>N_1</m> such that for every <m>n \geq N_1</m> we have <m>|a_n - L| &lt; \epsilon</m>. Similarly, since <m>a_n \to M</m> there exists an <m>N_2</m> such that for every <m>n \geq N_2</m> we have <m>|a_n - M| &lt; \epsilon</m>.
				</p>

				<p>
					We wish to make use of both of these inequalities simultaneously. So we set <m>N = \max(N_1, N_2)</m>. Now for every <m>n \geq N</m> we can say <me>\begin{aligned}
|L - M| &amp;= |L  - a_n + a_n - M| \\
&amp;\leq |L  - a_n| + |a_n - M|, \text{ by the triangle inequality}\\
&amp;&lt; \epsilon + \epsilon \\
&amp;= |L - M| / 2 + |L - M| / 2 \\
&amp;= |L - M| .
\end{aligned}</me>
				</p>

				<p>
					This chain of relations has established that for all <m>n \geq N</m> <me>|L - M| &lt;  |L - M| .</me> But this last statement is a clear contradiction as a real number cannot be strictly less than itself.
				</p>

				<p>
					So we conclude that the assumption that led to this conclusion must itself be false, i.e. that <m>L</m> and <m>M</m> are indeed the same. This proves the result. ◻
				</p>
    </proof>
  </theorem>
  
 

</section>